CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure is a schematic array of circles arranged in six vertical columns, with arrows drawn among them and part of the field sitting on a gray background. Five columns hold five circles each, evenly stacked from top to bottom, while the rightmost column holds only three. In every full column the circle in the middle position is an open white disk and the remaining circles are filled solid black. The white disk in the third column from the left carries the italic label x. Straight vertical arrows within each full column run upward from lower circles to the middle disk and from that disk to the circle above, and long curved arrows arc from a column's bottom circle to its topmost one. Along the row of white disks, horizontal arrows point inward toward x: the disks in the fourth through sixth columns send arrows leftward into x, with farther ones curving above intervening columns, and x in turn sends arrows leftward to the disks in the second and first columns. A rounded gray region with stepped edges covers the two circles below x in its own column together with the white disk and every circle beneath it in each column to the right. Because each arrow encodes an ordering between two elements, the shaded block gathers the elements the arrow chains place on one side of x, turning a counting argument about the quality of a partition into a visible area.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Six vertical columns of circles, each column representing one group of 5 elements sorted top-to-bottom (smallest at top, largest at bottom)
  - Columns 1-5: five circles each at rows 1..5
  - Column 6 (rightmost): only three circles, at rows 2, 3, 4 (a partial group)
  - In every column the row-3 (middle) circle is an open white disk with black outline = that group's median
  - All other circles are filled solid black
  - Italic serif label 'x' placed just below-right of the white disk in column 3 (the median-of-medians)
  - Straight vertical arrow in each column from row-4 black circle up to the white disk (row 3)
  - Curved arrow in each column arcing from the row-5 bottom circle up into the white disk (row 3)
  - Straight vertical arrow in each column from the white disk (row 3) up to the row-2 black circle
  - Long curved arrow in columns 1-5 arcing from the row-5 bottom circle all the way up to the row-1 top circle, bowing outward to the left of the column
  - Horizontal leftward arrow from the white disk of column 4 into x (column 3)
  - Long curved leftward arrows from the white disks of columns 5 and 6 arcing above the intervening disks into x
  - Horizontal leftward arrow from x to the white disk of column 2
  - Long curved leftward arrow from x arcing over column 2 into the white disk of column 1
  - Rounded-corner stepped gray region covering: the two black circles below x in column 3, the white disk plus the two circles below it in columns 4 and 5, and the white disk plus the circle below it in column 6
